  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Friday...\n\nTemperatures will trend warmer each day today into Monday (highs \nin 80s to lower 90s by Monday) under mostly sunny to partly cloudy\nskies. While mostly dry conditions will prevail with lower \nhumidities during this time, a few showers and maybe a weak \nthunderstorm or two are possible each day, mainly near the \nmountains and foothills (up to a 30 percent chance of showers and \na 15 percent chance of lightning, greatest Monday). With this \nactivity, brief gusty winds may develop, with gusts generally \naround 30 to 40 mph.\n\nThe unseasonably warm temperatures this weekend into next week \nwill cause mountain snowmelt to resume. This will allow higher \nflows on area waterways. Smaller streams and creeks flowing out of\nthe mountains will see rises this weekend into early next week, \nwith larger rivers and streams further downstream seeing the \ngreatest rises more Tuesday through Friday. With this, no river \nand stream flooding is expected at this time. For more \ninformation, visit water.noaa.gov.\n\nTuesday into the second half of the week, the weather looks to \nbecome more active with daily chances of showers and thunderstorms\nover much of the area. This is due to a developing cutoff low \npressure system that is forecast to drop into and stall over the \nwestern United States as an omega block sets up over the central \nUnited States. While uncertainty remains, increased instability \nand much higher atmospheric moisture content (precipitable water \nvalues near 1 inch) may allow for some stronger, potential severe,\nthunderstorms and heavy rain. Increased cloud cover by the second\nhalf of the week may limit this potential though. Snow levels \nnear 10,000 to 12,000 feet may also cause some rain on snow in the\nhigh terrain, potentially resulting in enhanced snow melt. If \nthis occurs, additional rises on area waterways could be observed.\nHowever, the precipitation potential Tuesday through the second \nhalf of the week remains uncertain. Will continue to monitor this \npotential through the week. \n\nThe placement of the upper level low pressure system becomes very\nuncertain late Friday into weekend. Depending on where this \nsystem tracks into the weekend, daily chances of precipitation may\ncontinue into next weekend. Arends",
